From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 13:05:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 40/40] vc4gfx: find the fb plane by the pointer it carries

Walking the list at word 0 gave a one-word answer:

    [VC4HVS]  walking active list of ch1:
    [VC4HVS]   [0000] 0x80000000 END

A bare END. That channel draws its background colour and nothing else --
and the HVS says it is running: DISPSTATX mode is RUN and its line
counter advances between dumps (a0034290, a00383b6). Something is being
scanned out, and it is not this list.

So stop asking DISPLIST/DISPLACT where the list is and look for the one
thing a display list cannot fake: a plane whose PTR0 is our framebuffer.
The scan is read-only, bounded, and prints the first sixteen dlist words
alongside, which is the part a wrong answer would still be useful for.

It deliberately stays inside HVS_DLIST_WORDS. The DISPBASEX windows hint
at a region reaching ~20736 words (ch2 [0,2047], ch1 [2048,15503], ch0
[15504,20735] -- they tile, which is not a coincidence), but the far end
of that would be peripheral offset 0x416400, past the 0x6000 the SCALER
block is documented to occupy. Word 4084 is proven readable, since the
firmware's filter kernel comes back from there intact, so the scan stays
inside what the hardware has already answered for. Widening it is a
separate decision with its own risk, not a side effect of a diagnostic.
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.c
@@ -1131,6 +1131,40 @@
         }
     }
 
+    /*
+     * Where is the plane that points at our framebuffer?
+     *
+     * On this port the HDMI channel is running (DISPSTATX mode = RUN, the
+     * line counter advances between dumps) with a display list whose head
+     * word is a bare END -- it draws background and nothing else, which is
+     * not what the screen shows. A display list can only be found by the
+     * pointer it carries, so look for that pointer directly rather than
+     * trusting DISPLIST/DISPLACT to name the head. Read-only, and bounded.
+     */
+    {
+        ULONG want = fb_phys & ~HVS_PTR_BUS_ALIAS;
+        ULONG i, hits = 0;
+
+        for (i = 0; i < HVS_DLIST_SCAN_WORDS && hits < 8; i++)
+        {
+            ULONG w = hvs_rd(HVS_DLIST_START + 4 * i);
+
+            if (w != 0 && (w & ~HVS_PTR_BUS_ALIAS) == want)
+            {
+                bug("[VC4HVS] scan: fb pointer 0x%08x at word %u\n", w, i);
+                hits++;
+            }
+        }
+        if (!hits)
+            bug("[VC4HVS] scan: no pointer to 0x%08x in %u words of dlist RAM\n",
+                fb_phys, (ULONG)HVS_DLIST_SCAN_WORDS);
+
+        bug("[VC4HVS] scan: words 0..15:");
+        for (i = 0; i < 16; i++)
+            bug(" %08x", hvs_rd(HVS_DLIST_START + 4 * i));
+        bug("\n");
+    }
+
     pv_dump("PV0", VC4_PV0_BASE);
     pv_dump("PV1", VC4_PV1_BASE);
     pv_dump("PV2", VC4_PV2_BASE);
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.h
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_hvs.h
@@ -67,6 +67,16 @@
 #define HVS_DLIST_START         0x2000
 #define HVS_DLIST_WORDS         4096
 
+/* How far the diagnostic scan looks for the live fb plane. Kept at the
+ * known-readable size deliberately: the DISPBASEX windows hint at a
+ * region reaching ~20736 words, which would put the far end at
+ * peripheral offset 0x416400, past the 0x6000 the SCALER block is
+ * documented to occupy. Word 4084 is proven readable (the firmware's
+ * filter kernel comes back from there intact), so this stays inside what
+ * the hardware has already answered for. Widening it is a separate
+ * decision, not a side effect of a diagnostic. */
+#define HVS_DLIST_SCAN_WORDS    HVS_DLIST_WORDS
+
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
 /* Display list entry, control word 0                                  */
 /* Validated fb plane: 0x47007817 = VALID, 7 words, order 3,           */
